======================================================================
Part 1: differentiate the CITED KEY identity W = Psi - eps*Psi_x
        in x, symbolically
======================================================================
W(x,y)   = Psi - eps*Psi_x  =
      ∂                    
- eps⋅──(Ψ(x, y)) + Ψ(x, y)
      ∂x                   

W_x(x,y) = d/dx[Psi - eps*Psi_x] =
       2                        
      ∂              ∂          
- eps⋅───(Ψ(x, y)) + ──(Ψ(x, y))
        2            ∂x         
      ∂x                        

Coefficient of Psi_xx (second x-derivative of Psi) in W_x: -eps
CONFIRMED: W_x = Psi_x - eps*Psi_xx GENUINELY contains a second-derivative
term, with coefficient exactly -eps (nonzero for eps>0) -- not a spurious
or cancelling artifact.

======================================================================
Part 2: consequence for the naive Phi_x recursion
======================================================================
Phi_x(x,y) = (1/eps) int_0^y e^{-v/eps} W_x(x+v,y-v) dv
           = (1/eps) int_0^y e^{-v/eps} [Psi_x(x+v,y-v) - eps*Psi_xx(x+v,y-v)] dv

This requires bounding Psi_xx (a SECOND x-derivative of Psi) directly,
via THIS route -- something no closed-form bound is cited for anywhere
in the record (only Psi and Psi_x-level quantities have established
bounds: M_Psi<=M_Phi via s03; the (star-star) oscillation bound of
DISC-DEC-100 Sec 5; no Psi_xx bound is cited or established).

This is EXACTLY the 'derivative loss' phenomenon already named in the
record: DISC-DEC-100 Sec 8.4 ('differentiating (BB-Psi') in x requires
control of d_x(Delta Phi), not merely Delta Phi -- an honest derivative
loss obstruction') and DISC-DEC-134's whole Sec 5 machinery (the (DX-K)
identity), which was built SPECIFICALLY to sidestep ever forming a
literal Phi_xx/Psi_xx term, by instead differentiating the FULL Volterra
equation (VOLTERRA-Phi) itself (an identity for Phi_y', not Phi_x) and
bounding the resulting forcing term via K_A^raw/M_y*N structure (no
second x-derivative anywhere in that route).

CONCLUSION (this script confirms symbolically, on the ORIGINAL PDE side
via KEY/E1/E2 directly, not merely by citing the derived-kernel side):
naive differentiation of (E2) in x to attack (C') directly hits the SAME
second-derivative obstruction that motivated the archive's entire
(DX-K)-based machinery (waves 29-30) -- there is no shortcut available
via this route that avoids that machinery's own work.

======================================================================
Part 3: for completeness -- does the SAME issue arise differentiating
        (E1) instead (the other cited exact identity)?
======================================================================
(E1): Psi_x = (x+y)*Psi - I.  Differentiating in x again:
  Psi_xx = Psi + (x+y)*Psi_x - I_x,   I_x(x,y) := int_0^y Phi_x(x,y')dy'

So Psi_xx (needed by Part 2) reduces to Psi, Psi_x, and I_x -- and I_x is
built from Phi_x itself (a FIRST derivative of Phi, i.e. exactly L_Phi(y')
-level content, for y'<=y). This CLOSES the loop back onto L_Phi -- i.e.
the naive route is genuinely SELF-REFERENTIAL at the derivative level
(L_Phi needs Psi_xx needs L_Phi again), not merely 'hard' -- a second,
independent confirmation (via (E1) instead of (BB-Psi')) of the same
derivative-loss obstruction.

Symbolic re-derivation of this specific consequence of (E1), confirming
no algebra error in the paragraph above:
        ∂                       ∂          
(x + y)⋅──(Ψ(x, y)) + Ψ(x, y) - ──(I(x, y))
        ∂x                      ∂x         
(= Psi(x,y) + (x+y)*Psi_x(x,y) - I_x(x,y), matching the paragraph above)
